page("rv-grilling-compact-charcoal-grill","RV Grilling With a Compact Charcoal Grill","Why RVers carry the flat-packing Slot Grill Compact: storage, campsite setup away from the rig, menus, and keeping carbon steel dry on the road.",IMG["c07"],f"""
<p>Storage is the number one problem in an RV. Every cubic inch matters, and most grills are awkward shapes that eat a whole bay. The Slot Grill Compact packs to about 1" thick, so it slides into a closet, under a mattress platform, behind a cushion or into a narrow storage bay. One RV owner bought it for exactly that reason:</p><blockquote>"The primary reason i purchased the grill was for the compactness when ready for storage in the RV's "closet". This grill is available for use in minutes and hardly takes up any space. Assembly takes only a minute. The design allows for use of charcoal or wood. I lit up the charcoal in minutes in windy conditions."<cite>Richard Shapero, Compact owner</cite></blockquote>
<h2>Why a flat-pack grill suits RV life</h2><ul><li><b>Packs about 1" flat</b>, with no legs or lids to rattle.</li><li><b>Tab and slot assembly</b> in 30–60 seconds, no tools, no hinges, nothing to lose under the rig.</li><li><b>Charcoal or wood</b>, so you can cook with campground firewood if allowed.</li><li><b>12-gauge carbon steel</b> that holds up to road vibration and years of use.</li><li><b>Made in Texas</b> and built and sold direct by the maker.</li></ul>
<h2>Setting up at the site</h2><ol><li>Check campground rules; many allow charcoal in designated areas or fire rings only, and burn bans override everything.</li><li>Set up well away from the RV, awning, slide-outs, propane tanks and tow vehicle, on gravel, a concrete pad or bare ground.</li><li>Never grill under the awning or inside the coach. Carbon monoxide from charcoal is deadly in enclosed spaces.</li><li>Keep the grill downwind of the rig so smoke doesn't drift into windows and vents.</li></ol>
<h2>Storage tips</h2><ul><li>Store only fully cold, clean, dry steel.</li><li>Put it in a bag or old box so soot stays off the closet.</li><li>Lightly oil cooking surfaces before storing for long drives; humidity in bays can cause surface rust. See <a href="/rust-prevention-carbon-steel-grill.html">rust prevention</a>.</li><li>Store charcoal in a sealed bin outside the living area.</li></ul>
<h2>RV menu ideas</h2><ul><li><a href="/campfire-breakfast-griddle.html">Griddle breakfast</a> with the <a href="{GRID}">Slot Grill griddle</a></li><li><a href="/reverse-sear-steak-small-grill.html">Ribeye for two</a></li><li><a href="/grilled-shrimp-compact-grill.html">Shrimp</a> and <a href="/grilled-vegetables-compact-grill.html">vegetables</a></li><li><a href="/smores-and-grilled-desserts-compact-grill.html">S'mores</a> after dinner where fires are allowed</li></ul>
<h2>Compact or Full Size for an RV?</h2><p>The Compact (about 14 lb, 146 sq in) suits couples and small families. If you regularly cook for a group at the campsite and have storage for it, the Full Size (about 27.6 lb, 231 sq in) is the bigger option. See <a href="/compact-vs-full-size-slot-grill.html">Compact vs. Full Size</a>.</p>
""","trips",[("Can I store a Slot Grill in my RV?","Yes. The Compact packs about 1 inch thick, so it fits in a closet, under a bed platform or in a narrow bay. Store it cold, clean, dry and lightly oiled."),("Can I use a charcoal grill under my RV awning?","No. Set it up well away from the RV, awning, slide-outs and propane, in the open. Never use a charcoal grill inside or under cover."),("Does the Compact work with campground firewood?","Slot Grill lists charcoal or cooking wood as fuel. Use untreated wood and follow campground rules."),("How much does the Slot Grill Compact weigh?","About 14 lb, grill only.")])

page("beach-grilling-compact-grill","Beach Grilling With a Compact Charcoal Grill","How to grill on the beach with the Slot Grill Compact: rules, setting up on sand, wind, salt air care, menus and disposing of coals safely.",IMG["c13"],f"""
<p>A beach cookout is a great day, but beaches are hard on grills and hard on fires: wind, sand, salt and strict rules. The Slot Grill Compact handles it well. It sits solid on leveled sand, packs about 1" flat so it rides with the umbrellas, and the 2⅞" air gap under the coal bed helps minimize radiant heat to the surface below.</p>
<h2>Check the rules first</h2><p>Beach fire and grill rules vary widely. Some beaches allow charcoal grills, some only in designated areas or pits, and many ban them entirely. Some require coals to go into specific hot-coal bins. Check with the beach, park or city before you go.</p>
<h2>Setting up on sand</h2><ol><li>Pick a spot away from dune grass, umbrellas, towels and foot traffic.</li><li>Level a patch of firm, damp-ish sand so the grill sits flat.</li><li>Assemble and check it's stable.</li><li>Position so the wind isn't blowing smoke and sparks at your group or neighbors.</li><li>Keep a bucket of water nearby.</li></ol>
<h2>Wind and sand</h2><ul><li>Beach wind makes coals burn faster and hotter on the windward side. Plan more fuel and point the hot end into the wind.</li><li>Light with a chimney; it's far more reliable in wind than lighter fluid.</li><li>Keep food covered until it goes on the grill so sand doesn't blow in.</li><li>Don't cook in very high wind; sparks travel.</li></ul>
<h2>Beach menu ideas</h2><ul><li><a href="/grilled-shrimp-compact-grill.html">Garlic shrimp</a> and <a href="/grilled-fish-compact-grill.html">fish tacos</a></li><li><a href="/smash-burgers-on-a-griddle.html">Smash burgers</a> on the <a href="{GRID}">griddle</a></li><li><a href="/sausage-and-peppers-compact-grill.html">Sausages</a></li><li>Grilled pineapple from the <a href="/smores-and-grilled-desserts-compact-grill.html">dessert guide</a></li></ul>
<h2>Never bury coals</h2><p>Buried coals stay hot for many hours and cause serious burns to bare feet. Let coals burn down, drown them with water and stir, then pack the cold ash out in a metal can or put it in a designated hot-coal bin.</p>
<h2>Salt air care</h2><p>Salt air and spray speed up rust on raw carbon steel. After a beach day, rinse sand and salt off once the grill is cold, dry every piece thoroughly, and re-oil the cooking surfaces with oil or <a href="{BALM}">Seasoning Balm</a>. See <a href="/rust-prevention-carbon-steel-grill.html">rust prevention</a>.</p>
""","trips",[("Can I grill on the beach?","It depends on the beach. Some allow charcoal grills, some only in designated areas, and many ban them. Check local rules first."),("How do I set up a grill on sand?","Level a firm patch of sand, set the grill flat, check it's stable, and keep it away from dune grass and foot traffic."),("Can I bury hot coals in the sand?","No. Buried coals stay hot for hours and cause severe burns. Drown and stir them, then pack out cold ash or use a hot-coal bin."),("Does salt air rust carbon steel?","Yes, it speeds up surface rust. Rinse, dry thoroughly and re-oil after beach use.")])

page("small-patio-balcony-charcoal-grilling","Small Patio and Backyard Grilling With a Compact Grill","Grilling on a small patio or backyard with the Slot Grill Compact: placement, clearances, protecting tables and pavers, smoke, storage and rules for apartments.",IMG["c07"],f"""
<p>Not everyone has room for a big grill. If your outdoor space is a small patio, a corner of a backyard or a concrete pad, a compact charcoal grill gives you real charcoal cooking without giving up the space full-time. The Slot Grill Compact has a 17.5" × 10.25" footprint, stands 10.5" tall, and packs away about 1" flat when you're done.</p>
<h2>Check the rules (especially for apartments)</h2><p>Many apartments, condos, HOAs and local fire codes restrict or ban charcoal grills on balconies and near buildings. Check your lease, HOA rules and local fire code before you grill. If charcoal isn't allowed on your balcony, it isn't allowed; use a permitted common area instead.</p>
<h2>Where to put it</h2><ul><li>On a stable, level, non-combustible surface: concrete, pavers, stone or gravel.</li><li>In the open, away from siding, fences, overhangs, eaves, umbrellas, furniture cushions and plants.</li><li>Never in a garage, carport, under a covered patio or near windows or vents where smoke can enter.</li></ul>
<h2>Protecting tables and surfaces</h2><p>The Compact holds the coal bed 2⅞" above the table, and that air gap helps keep radiant heat off tables, tailgates and decks. It's still a charcoal fire, so use a non-combustible surface or a fire-safe pad underneath, and don't set it on plastic, glass, painted or wooden tables. Ash and sparks can still fall.</p>
<h2>Managing smoke for neighbors</h2><ul><li>Use a chimney instead of lighter fluid; it's less smoky and smelly once lit.</li><li>Wait for coals to ash over before cooking; half-lit charcoal smokes most.</li><li>Trim excess fat and use a <a href="/two-zone-fire-on-a-small-grill.html">two-zone fire</a> to control flare-ups.</li><li>Grill when the wind isn't blowing straight at a neighbor's window.</li></ul>
<h2>Weeknight patio menu</h2><ul><li><a href="/smash-burgers-on-a-griddle.html">Smash burgers</a> (fast, half chimney)</li><li><a href="/pork-chops-small-charcoal-grill.html">Pork chops</a></li><li><a href="/grilled-vegetables-compact-grill.html">Vegetables</a> and <a href="/grilled-shrimp-compact-grill.html">shrimp</a></li><li><a href="/chicken-wings-small-charcoal-grill.html">Wings</a> for game night</li></ul>
<h2>Storage between cooks</h2><p>If you leave it assembled, once ash is completely cold, pull one side panel, empty it, and slide it back. Keep it covered or under shelter from rain, or pack it flat and store it inside a shed. Keep the cooking surfaces lightly oiled. See <a href="/how-to-clean-and-store-a-compact-grill.html">clean and store</a>.</p>
""","trips",[("Can I use a charcoal grill on an apartment balcony?","Often not. Many leases, HOAs and fire codes ban charcoal on balconies. Check the rules before grilling."),("How much space does the Slot Grill Compact need?","Its footprint is 17.5 by 10.25 inches and it stands 10.5 inches tall, but leave generous clearance from anything that can burn."),("Can I put the Slot Grill on a table?","The 2⅞ inch air gap helps minimize radiant heat to surfaces below, but use a stable non-combustible surface or fire-safe pad, not plastic, glass, painted or wooden tables."),("How do I reduce charcoal smoke for neighbors?","Light with a chimney, let coals ash over before cooking, trim fat, and control flare-ups with a two-zone fire.")])

page("power-outage-emergency-cooking-charcoal-grill","Power Outage and Emergency Cooking on a Charcoal Grill","Cook safely outdoors during power outages and after storms with the Slot Grill Compact: carbon monoxide rules, fuel storage, fridge food safety, and boiling water.",IMG["c28"],f"""
<p>When the power goes out after a storm, a charcoal grill becomes the kitchen. The Slot Grill Compact stores flat, needs no electricity, gas or tools, burns charcoal or cooking wood, and sets up in about a minute. Here's how to use it safely in an outage and what to keep on hand.</p>
<div class="note"><b>Most important rule:</b> never use a charcoal grill indoors, in a garage (even with the door open), on a covered porch, in a carport, tent, camper or vehicle, or near windows, doors or vents. Burning charcoal produces carbon monoxide, which is colorless, odorless and deadly. Carbon monoxide poisoning is a leading cause of death after storms. Use working battery-powered CO alarms indoors.</div>
<h2>Build an outage cooking kit</h2><ul><li>Slot Grill Compact (and a <a href="{GRID}">griddle</a> for pans of food)</li><li>Charcoal stored dry in a lidded bin; enough for several days of cooking</li><li>Chimney starter, newspaper, natural starters, multiple lighters and matches in a waterproof container</li><li>Tongs, spatula, gloves, headlamp or flashlight</li><li><a href="{THERMO}">Instant-read thermometer</a> (battery)</li><li>Heavy-duty foil, foil pans, a pot or kettle for boiling water</li><li>Metal ash can with lid</li><li>Cooler(s) and a way to get ice</li></ul>
<h2>Fridge and freezer food safety</h2><p>U.S. food safety guidance: a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours if unopened; a full freezer holds about 48 hours (24 if half full) if the door stays closed. Keep doors closed as much as possible. Use a thermometer: discard perishables that have been above 40°F for 2 hours or more. Cook thawing meat first, and cook it to safe temperatures. When in doubt, throw it out.</p>
<h2>Cooking order in an outage</h2><ol><li>Refrigerated perishables first (meat, dairy-based dishes).</li><li>Then thawing freezer items.</li><li>Then pantry foods and canned goods (heat in a pot on the grate or opened cans on the griddle, not sealed cans).</li></ol>
<h2>Boiling water</h2><p>If authorities issue a boil-water advisory, follow their instructions. A pot on the grate over a strong bed of coals can bring water to a rolling boil. Cooking over charcoal is fully outdoors, so set up where you can watch it safely.</p>
<h2>Setup after a storm</h2><ul><li>Look for downed power lines, debris and standing water first. Stay well away from lines.</li><li>Set up in the open, on a non-combustible surface, away from the house and anything that burns.</li><li>Don't grill in high winds.</li></ul>
<h2>Store it ready</h2><p>Keep the grill clean, dry, lightly oiled and stacked flat with your kit, so it's ready when you need it. Rotate charcoal so it stays dry.</p>
""","trips",[("Can I use a charcoal grill in my garage during a power outage?","No. Never use a charcoal grill indoors, in a garage, carport, covered porch, tent or vehicle, even with doors open. Carbon monoxide can kill."),("How long is food safe in the fridge without power?","About 4 hours if the door stays closed. A full freezer holds about 48 hours (24 if half full). Discard perishables above 40°F for 2 hours or more."),("Can I boil water on a charcoal grill?","Yes, a pot on the grate over a strong bed of coals can reach a rolling boil. Follow any official boil-water instructions."),("What should be in an outage cooking kit?","A charcoal grill, dry charcoal, a chimney and starters, lighters, tools, a thermometer, foil, a pot, a headlamp and a metal ash can.")])
